@* Ported from Reports/RetainedEarningsPopup.ascx - the retained-earnings statement shown
   CHROME-FREE inside the pop-up window BalanceSheet's retained-earning drill-down opens.

   ⚠ THIS .ascx HAD NO CODE-BEHIND OF ITS OWN: it declared
       CodeBehind="RetainedEarnings.ascx.cs"
       Inherits="MixERP.Net.Core.Modules.Finance.Reports.RetainedEarnings"
   i.e. it SHARED Reports/RetainedEarnings' class, and there is no RetainedEarningsPopup.ascx.cs
   or .designer.cs in the repository. Treating it as "a control with no code-behind" would have
   silently emptied this screen. The shared implementation is the
   Internal/RetainedEarningsStatement component below, which both screens render.

   WHAT THIS SCREEN ACTUALLY DIFFERS IN, and all of it is preserved:
     * MasterPageId = "MixERPBlankMaster.Master" - this is what renders it without the
       application chrome inside the popup window. Assigned in the CONSTRUCTOR, because
       ModuleHost.razor reads it off an Activator.CreateInstance PROBE INSTANCE on which no
       lifecycle method ever runs (and it is what selects the host's BlankLayout).
     * the <div style="padding: 24px;"> wrapper the .ascx put around the placeholder.
     * NO <script>: this .ascx carried none, so its #PrintButton has always been inert. It is
       deliberately left inert - loading the sibling's script would newly switch a button on.
   RemoveTheme was NOT set on this control (unlike the Reports/*Report screens), so it is not
   set here either.

   The original declared no OverridePath, AccessLevel or IsLandingPage override and implemented
   no ITransaction. *@

@inherits MixERP.Net.Framework.Controls.MixERPUserControl
@using MixERP.Net.Core.Modules.Finance.Internal

<div style="padding: 24px;">
    <RetainedEarningsStatement />
</div>

@code {
    public RetainedEarningsPopup()
    {
        //<%@ Control ... MasterPageId="MixERPBlankMaster.Master" %>
        this.MasterPageId = "MixERPBlankMaster.Master";
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     MixERPUserControlBase.OnControlLoad is abstract, so every ported control declares it.
    ///     The legacy body (shared with Reports/RetainedEarnings) built the screen that is now
    ///     the Internal/RetainedEarningsStatement component above, whose own lifecycle runs the
    ///     asynchronous catalog lookup and the first bind.
    /// </summary>
    public override void OnControlLoad(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
    }
}
